  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/5/53/X-Men_Vol_1_4.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The first three pages are filled with Professor X testing first the Beast, then Iceman before pulling out a \"birthday\" cake to celebrate them being together one year.\n\nBut the scene then shifts to another table, where we get our first glimpse of the Brotherhood of Evil Mutants, as Mastermind spars with the Toad, then the Scarlet Witch and finally Quicksilver.&nbsp; Their leader Magneto is elsewhere, securing a freighter from the office of a large shipping line, and it doesn't take him long to gain control over it with the help of his magnetic power.\n\nNot long afterward, the freighter is spotted by a soaring Angel while out on patrol.&nbsp; He takes note of it -- primarily because it is seemingly-abandoned -- and reports its presence back to the Professor, who feels that something is off.&nbsp; An unmanned freighter strikes him as \"ominous.\"\n\nWhen Magneto returns to his \"uncharted island\" headquarters in the Atlantic Ocean with the new destroyer and learns that his assembled team has been bickering, he reminds Quicksilver and the Scarlet Witch what they owe him.&nbsp; It seems they're only serving him to repay some sort of debt (that only Wanda owes him).&nbsp; Magneto reminds her that that he rescued her from a mob of angry villagers in the heart of Europe, who called her a \"scarlet witch\" because of her mutant powers.&nbsp; He reminds them that they are \"homo superior\" and that they were born to \"rule the Earth!\"&nbsp; Quicksilver isn't sold on it, but as long as his sister stays with Magneto, he will too, as he has \"no love for humans either.\"\n\nDays later, Xavier reads in the paper about \"one lone freighter attacking an entire South American nation\" (by the name of Santo Marco).&nbsp; He senses that evil mutants must be responsible.&nbsp; He summons his X-Men, but they find him in a trance, mentally in touch with Magneto, who can't understand why he opposes them.&nbsp; The humans must be made <i>slaves</i> and are not worthy to share dominion of the Earth.&nbsp; Very well then -- they will be mortal enemies!&nbsp; So Professor X knows that Magneto is about to launch an all-out offensive against mankind.\n\nSoon, off the coast of Santo Marco, Magneto and Mastermind are able to fool the city into surrendering, allowing Magneto to form an <i>actual</i> army to serve him, and in short order, he institutes martial law and closes the country's borders.\n\nIt isn't long before a car rolls up to the border, apparently containing \"an American professor and some students\" who arrive on a good-will visit from America.&nbsp; For some reason, Magneto is eager to show such people that \"he is a kind and beloved ruler,\" and they are allowed to enter the country.&nbsp; (<i>Why on Earth would he care what the world filled with humans thinks</i>?)&nbsp; Magneto can sense Xavier's mental presence and knows that the X-Men are close by!\n\nFrom where they're staying inside the \"imperial palace,\" Professor X now realizes that Magneto isn't the only mutant here, making their entire mission more difficult.&nbsp; Magneto has let them come to him, \"like flies entering a spider's trap,\" and he begins to form a plan of attack.\n\nThe Beast goes out first, but as he creeps into position, the Toad engages him before Mastermind steps in and fools the Beast into thinking he's falling.&nbsp; On the other side of the castle, Angel strikes, but quick as he is, he's not faster than Quicksilver!&nbsp; But QS is maneuvered into a wall before the Scarlet Witch appears to see what he's done to her brother (complete with the Angel taking one look at her and thinking \"<i>Wow!</i>  What a dish!&nbsp; If <i>she's</i> an evil mutant, I want an application blank!\" <i>omg!</i>)&nbsp; The Scarlet Witch drops the ceiling on him, and presto, the Angel is now a prisoner!\n\nCyclops then arrives and blasts the electric generator, which sends voltage throughout the castle!&nbsp; The evil mutants must run for it, leaving Scott there to free the Angel.&nbsp; He has no choice but to blast the generator right through the side of the building to keep it from harming them.&nbsp; The others join them finally.\n\nAt the same time, Magneto is preparing a small bomb to destroy the X-Men and a larger nuclear bomb to wipe Santo Marco off the face of Earth.&nbsp; Quicksilver can't believe that he would destroy all the innocent people just to facilitate his escape!\n\nThe X-Men then arrive and avoid being blown to bits, thanks to Professor X hurling himself out of his chair to block Hank from going through the booby-trapped door, but the smaller blast starts the 10 minute countdown for the nuclear bomb, and they now must all flee!&nbsp; But Quicksilver can't let the larger bomb explode and pauses long enough to run back to get it.&nbsp; He won't allow the bomb to destroy a nation, but he warns the X-Men that they are betrayers of homo superior and that they are still enemies.\n\nThe X-Men gather around their leader who tells them that his mental power was deadened by the small explosion.&nbsp; He says to pursue them, but they stay with him, but now that he has no power, they'll be on their <i>own</i> the next time.&nbsp; They must prove <i>worthy</i> of all the training they've received.\n\nWill they be strong enough?&nbsp; That's the question we're left with!",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: left | BirthdayCake | width=12%] The occasion of the school's one-year anniversary was a decent way to start things off, as it clarifies exactly how much time has passed since the title's first issue.&nbsp; (Or at least I <i>think</i> it does -- the Professor isn't clear whether the anniversary is from then, as opposed to when he first opened the school.)\n\n[ASSET: right | FourNewMutants | width=12%] Whatever the case, it's presumably been <i>months</i> since [LINK:1963:XMEN_1|XMEN #1|2026-11-25], which certainly has afforded time for Magneto (who made his first appearance in that issue) to have rescued the siblings (Pietro and Wanda, their names provided on pages 4 and 5 respectively) from the angry village in Europe and assemble his Brotherhood of Evil Mutants.\n\nThough the \"leader\" the four newcomers keep mentioning in the early moments isn't revealed as Magneto until page 6, it doesn't come as a surprise to readers at <i>all</i>, particularly since the cover of the magazine pretty much spells it out, showing the Master of Magnetism <b>dominating</b> the image and boldly announcing that the story features the [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=Brotherhood of Evil Mutants] (and we can assume we've just been introduced to all of them here).\n\nWhen Magneto first appears, he is \"recognized\" by the shipping line office employee, which is initially surprising since, as far as we know, Magneto has only appeared once before, and this to only an army facility back in issue 1 (Cape Citadel), but as he did announce his coming with skywriting (see its page 13), we can assume some member of the press saw it, or took a photo, and announced his presence to the world.&nbsp; (Let's face it:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=he wasn't exactly <i>subtle</i>!])\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe newly-introduced characters are quite a mix.\n\n[HANG0] * The Toad is sniveling, but it seems obvious enough that is to be his role.\n[HANG0] * Mastermind is cruel and enjoys toying with his victims\n[HANG0] * The siblings are obviously <i>not</i> buying everything that Magneto is selling, but they owe him a debt and so are serving him, at least for the moment.\n\n[ASSET: left | AnUnpaidDebt | width=22%] It is this last pair that prove to be the most interesting, because Magneto himself recounts how he saved Wanda's life from a group of \"superstitious villagers\" in Europe (though I note in the flashback she is called a \"[SPAN: severity=error; text=Scarlet] Witch\" by them, even though there is not a <i>shred</i> of scarlet clothes on her body -- <i>very odd</i>).\n[BR]\n[BR] Be that as it may, the issue suggests it is <i>Wanda</i> who owes Magneto this \"debt\" and that Pietro is only sticking around to be with his sister.&nbsp; (One is forced to wonder where <i>he</i> was when the villagers were attacking his sister, but let's not get <i>too</i> nit-picky...)\n\nThe long and short of it is that <i>neither</i> of them seem happy with Magneto's desire to \"rule the Earth\" but also have no love of <i>humans</i>, so for the moment, they're present.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=As a reader, even just this short bit of characterization is enough to elicit <i>some</i> small amount of empathy for this pair.]&nbsp; Not only that, but Stan sells their participation quite well by having Quicksilver add, \"Why <i>should</i> we love the homo sapiens??&nbsp; They hate us -- fear us because of our superior power!\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=If the debt weren't motivation <i>enough</i>, the humans' distrust of mutants is additional incentive!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(This is textured characterization, and to that, I say] [SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=small; fonttype=bold; text=well done!)]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But to artist Jack Kirby, I also have to point out that Wanda's horrible wrap-around tiara is nothing less than a <i>crime against fashion</i>!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | MentalMeeting | width=12%] The inevitable conflict between the X-Men and the new Brotherhood of Evil Mutants really kicks off on page 10 when the Professor senses Magneto's thoughts, and the two argue over the fate of humanity, <b>clearly in disagreement</b> of how humanity is to be treated.\n\nWhy Magneto chooses the small South American nation of Santo Marco is not entirely clear, but we can perhaps assume he targeted it as a small test case.&nbsp; His previous attempt to take over Cape Citadel didn't work out (as seen in [LINK:1963:XMEN_1|XMEN #1|2026-11-25]), so now that he has allies, one can only suppose that he's trying to conquer a small nation, away from the United States, using it as some sort of \"proof of concept.\"\n\nRegardless, the \"proud people off Santo Marcos\" prove to be no match for the Brotherhood, and they quickly succumb.\n\n[ASSET: right | KindBelovedRuler | width=17%] What makes far <i>less</i> sense is the motivation behind Magneto seeming to care about whether he's a \"kind and beloved ruler\" (which the guard in the panel thinks when the \"American professor and some students\" drive up).&nbsp; It <i>may</i> have simply been some kind of cover story told to this border guard so that he would let the Professor and his students pass.\n[BR]\n[BR] Even in only his second appearance, Magneto's style belies this sentiment.&nbsp; He doesn't strike one as the sort to care one <i>iota</i> how the world perceives him, so [SPAN: severity=warning; text=I find it hard to buy this!]\n[BR]\n[BR] To me, what seems <i>more</i> likely is that this is just an ill-conceived comment randomly thrown in by Stan, and because he serves as both writer <i>and</i> editor, they just slip through the cracks, such as the one in the <i>previous</i> issue, which was a <i>legendary</i> face plant (see my [LINK:1963:XMEN_3|XMEN #3|2027-03-10] review).\n\n\n\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\nNeither of them were happy with Magneto's callous choice to simply destroy the nation\n\n\n\n\nThe story gets a full mid-grade rating more for the introduction of the four new evil mutants than it does for the overall plot.&nbsp; Magneto's conquest of a small nation to rule seems rushed -- was a South American country really a good first choice?&nbsp; The X-Men seemed to be right on top of it, even before he'd finished establishing himself as ruler, and Magneto did not seem well-prepared for their arrival.&nbsp; (Plus, where did he get hold of a nuclear bomb anyway?&nbsp; I'm sure they're not just sitting around in the hulls of any freighters, even if he <i>did</i> steal it!)\n\n\n\nThe nonsense about Magneto caring about whether he's a benevolent leader may have simply been a cover story told to the border guard so that he would let the Professor and his students pass.&nbsp; I don't buy that it was a real desire at all.&nbsp; These sorts of comments thrown at random into the stories are sometimes ill-conceived, but one suspects that because Stan Lee was both writer and editor, they slipped through (such as the one in the previous issue, which was legendary).\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis is borderline mid-grade.&nbsp; Not at all a <i>terrible</i> story, but hardly something that goes beyond just \"Fine.\"\n\nInteresting that the Scarlet Witch is drawn with a green costume on the cover -- hardly a single thing <i>scarlet</i> about her outside her hair color, and Pietro's outfit is mis-colored as well.&nbsp; I can <i>sort</i> of understand the desire not to put red next to red on the cover (Magneto's uniform next to the Scarlet Witch), but one might respond to this objection by pointing out that her actual <i>name</i> pretty much declares the color.&nbsp; There isn't a lot of wiggle room here!\n\nThe Beast once again has gloves on in the earlier part of the book, which disappear later on, and then finally re-appear on the 4<sup>th</sup>-to-last panel!&nbsp; (In fact, page 22 shows them on and off again even <i>between</i> panels!)&nbsp; Someone is confused... having too many characters dressed in the same costumes can lead to these types of mistakes.",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] It seems that South American countries were on Stan's mind this month, as [LINK:1964:TTA_54_giant0|TTA #54|2027-05-01] has \"Santo Rico,\" whereas this issue has \"Santo Marco.\"&nbsp; Both of these issues were published on the same day."
  ],
  "decoder_ring": "As a follow-up on my announcement in my XMEN #1 review that the name \"Magneto\" is correctly pronounced as \"magnet\" and \"o\" -- page 15 has the Angel proclaim (in a sing-song voice):&nbsp; \"Oh <b>Mageeeeeeto</b> --where <i>are</i> you??\"&nbsp; This was <i>clearly</i> done in a mocking tone, very much the same way that Ben has called the Sub-Mariner the \"Sub-Mar<b>EEEE</b>ner\" -- clearly saying it wrong... <i>on purpose</i>.",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}